...
4067   http = cloud_http(parsed, "GET", NULL, NULL, 0, (char *)buf,
4068                     max_bytes + 2, &got);
```

Because `parsed->host`/`parsed->port` come from the download URL, `cloud_keep_take()`'s `strcmp` at 1026-1027 fails against the API's cached host and the code opens a fresh socket — the cross-host reuse you were worried about cannot happen. `parsed->tls` also comes from the URL's scheme, so an `https://` minutes URL goes through `vela_https_request()` and never touches `keep_sock`, while a `http://` one goes through the same one-slot cache. Also note `cloud_http()` passes `headers = NULL` here, so no `Accept`-negotiation or auth headers on downloads, and the buffer is `max_bytes + 2` so an overrun is detectable (`got > max_bytes` → `-EFBIG` at 4055-4062).

The practical consequence of the single slot: within one session the sequence open → register → FDS PUT → poll → ... alternates hosts, so the API socket is torn down and reopened around every payload transfer on the cleartext path. The host/port check keeps that *correct*; it just means the "pin the session to one instance" property described in the struct comment (309-330) survives only between consecutive same-host requests, not across an upload or a download.

